{"continue":{"clcontinue":"150701502|1974_AMC_Javelin","continue":"||imageinfo"},"query":{"pages":{"64093778":{"pageid":64093778,"ns":6,"title":"File:1968 Mercury Cougar 2 door Hardtop (37369144146).jpg","index":1,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":4805761,"width":5184,"height":3456,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/1968_Mercury_Cougar_2_door_Hardtop_%2837369144146%29.jpg/960px-1968_Mercury_Cougar_2_door_Hardtop_%2837369144146%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":960,"thumbheight":640,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/1968_Mercury_Cougar_2_door_Hardtop_%2837369144146%29.jpg/1920px-1968_Mercury_Cougar_2_door_Hardtop_%2837369144146%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/1968_Mercury_Cougar_2_door_Hardtop_%2837369144146%29.jpg/960px-1968_Mercury_Cougar_2_door_Hardtop_%2837369144146%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":960,"height":640,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/1968_Mercury_Cougar_2_door_Hardtop_%2837369144146%29.jpg/1920px-1968_Mercury_Cougar_2_door_Hardtop_%2837369144146%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/1968_Mercury_Cougar_2_door_Hardtop_%2837369144146%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1968_Mercury_Cougar_2_door_Hardtop_(37369144146).jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=64093778","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2017-11-15 22:32:04","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"1968 Mercury Cougar 2 door Hardtop (37369144146)","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation|Australia photographs taken on 2017-09-24|Flickr images reviewed by FlickreviewR 2|1968 Mercury Cougar|Photographs by Sicnag","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"GPSLatitude":{"value":"-34.819391","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSLongitude":{"value":"138.954595","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSMapDatum":{"value":"WGS-84","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<p>Edsel Ford introduced the Mercury in 1939 as a model to fill the gap between the basic Ford, and the luxury Lincoln.\nThe Mercury Cougar was based on Fords pony car, the Mustang from 1967-73, then the Ford Torino form 1974-80.\nThe first generation Cougar was built from 1967-70, it was 3 inches longer than the Mustang and more luxurious. \nIn 1967, it got a full-width divided 'electric shaver' grille with hidden headlamps. Only available as a two door Hardtop, it was available in base or the luxury XR-7 (woodgrain dash and steering wheel, centre and overhead consoles). Base engine was the 200hp 289 V8. The GT Performance package was available on both models (390 V8, upgraded suspension and better brakes)\nIn 1968, the Cougar got side marker lights, the 210hp 302 was now the base engine on XR-7s (289 still in the base model), the 335hp 428 was available on the GT. \n619 XR-7Gs were produced, any engine but they got hood scoop, fog lamps, and hood pins.\nThe new GT-E package; 357 built with the 390hp 427 V8 and 37 built with the 410hp 428SCJ.   \n</p>\n1969 saw a facelift, new model names and a convertible was available.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2017-09-24 13:33","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/42220226@N07/37369144146/\">1968 Mercury Cougar 2 door Hardtop</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/42220226@N07\">Sicnag</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY 2.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution 2.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"true","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseUrl":{"value":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Copyrighted":{"value":"True","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"cc-by-2.0","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}],"categories":[{"ns":14,"title":"Category:1968 Mercury Cougar"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:Australia photographs taken on 2017-09-24"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:CC-BY-2.0"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:Flickr images reviewed by FlickreviewR 2"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:Photographs by Sicnag"}]},"81848649":{"pageid":81848649,"ns":6,"title":"File:1968 Mercury Cougar XR-7 Coupe (32508937087).jpg","index":11,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":4422920,"width":5184,"height":3456,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/1968_Mercury_Cougar_XR-7_Coupe_%2832508937087%29.jpg/960px-1968_Mercury_Cougar_XR-7_Coupe_%2832508937087%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":960,"thumbheight":640,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/1968_Mercury_Cougar_XR-7_Coupe_%2832508937087%29.jpg/1920px-1968_Mercury_Cougar_XR-7_Coupe_%2832508937087%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/1968_Mercury_Cougar_XR-7_Coupe_%2832508937087%29.jpg/960px-1968_Mercury_Cougar_XR-7_Coupe_%2832508937087%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":960,"height":640,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/1968_Mercury_Cougar_XR-7_Coupe_%2832508937087%29.jpg/1920px-1968_Mercury_Cougar_XR-7_Coupe_%2832508937087%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/1968_Mercury_Cougar_XR-7_Coupe_%2832508937087%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1968_Mercury_Cougar_XR-7_Coupe_(32508937087).jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=81848649","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2019-09-03 17:45:15","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"1968 Mercury Cougar XR-7 Coupe (32508937087)","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation|Flickr images reviewed by FlickreviewR 2|Australia photographs taken on 2019-03-23|1968 Mercury Cougar|Photographs by Sicnag","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"GPSLatitude":{"value":"-38.039844","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSLongitude":{"value":"145.364398","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSMapDatum":{"value":"WGS-84","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<p>Edsel Ford introduced the Mercury in 1939 as a marque to fill the gap between the basic Ford, and the luxury Lincoln.\nThe Mercury Cougar was based on Fords pony car, the Mustang from 1967-73, then the Ford Torino form 1974-80.\nThe first generation Cougar was built from 1967-70, it was 3 inches longer than the Mustang and more luxurious. \nIn 1967, it got a full-width divided 'electric shaver' grille with hidden headlamps. Only available as a two door Hardtop, it was available in base or the luxury XR-7 (woodgrain dash and steering wheel, centre and overhead consoles). Base engine was the 200hp 289 V8. The GT Performance package was available on both models (390 V8, upgraded suspension and better brakes)\nIn 1968, the Cougar got side marker lights, the 210hp 302 was now the base engine on XR-7s (289 still in the base model), the 335hp 428 was available on the GT. \n619 XR-7Gs were produced, any engine but they got hood scoop, fog lamps, and hood pins.\nThe new GT-E package; 357 built with the 390hp 427 V8 and 37 built with the 410hp 428SCJ.   \n</p>\n1969 saw a facelift, new model names and a convertible was available.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2019-03-23 09:37","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/42220226@N07/32508937087/\">1968 Mercury Cougar XR-7 Coupe</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/42220226@N07\">Sicnag</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY 2.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution 2.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"true","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseUrl":{"value":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Copyrighted":{"value":"True","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"cc-by-2.0","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}],"categories":[{"ns":14,"title":"Category:1968 Mercury Cougar"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:Australia photographs taken on 2019-03-23"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:CC-BY-2.0"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:Flickr images reviewed by FlickreviewR 2"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:Photographs by Sicnag"}]},"150701502":{"pageid":150701502,"ns":6,"title":"File:1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 01of14.jpg","index":3,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":3670043,"width":4032,"height":3024,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":960,"thumbheight":720,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":960,"height":720,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/63/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=150701502","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2024-07-23 22:25:54","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 01of14","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Self-published work|Three-quarter views of AMC automobiles|Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation|Blue AMC coupes|AMC automobiles in the United States|Photographs by CZmarlin|1974 AMC Javelin|AMO 2024 convention","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"GPSLatitude":{"value":"37.234256","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSLongitude":{"value":"-77.403061","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSMapDatum":{"value":"WGS-84","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin - a \"Blue Diamond\" (paint code - E1) original with a matching interior in blue. 1974 was the last year this high-performance pony was built by American Motors Corporation. The automobile marketplace had changed with the Arab oil embargo and declining interest in high-performance vehicles. In 1974, Chrysler also abandoned the pony car market while the Ford Mustang became a four-cylinder version of the sub-compact Pinto, and GM downsized their standard engines. \n<p>This car has just over 6,000 miles! It is factory-fresh! Original features include AMC's 360 CID (5.9 L) V8 engine, center console with \"stirrup\" shifted automatic transmission, air conditioning, AM/FM stereo radio, and AMC's combination tachometer and clock (Tick-Tach) full instrumentation, sports-type three-spoke steering wheel, and 14-6-inch styled steel road wheels with F70x14 Goodyear Polyglass raised white letter tires. It has the enlarged bumper guard system required for cars sold in California, Georgia, Maryland, and North Carolina. 1974 was the first year of the 5-mph bumper standards, but the Javelin was exempt from incorporating the shock-absorbing bumper systems. \n</p><p>A natural \"diamond\" of a car! The rear  \"ducktail\" spoiler from the AMX model has been added to this car. This AMX design feature was not available on regular Javelins from the factory. Likewise, the Javelins had available \"Rally\" side stripes (optional without the full vinyl body-side scuff molding, which this car has), but the hood \"T\" stripe was available on only the AMX version. \n</p>\n<ul><li>The pictures were taken during the American Motors Owners Association (AMO) annual convention in June 2024, organized by the Potomac Ramblers Club with events held in historic Petersburg downtown by the rail station and at the Keystone Truck and Tractor Museum in Virginia.</li></ul>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2024-06-12 19:04:13","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<span class=\"int-own-work\" lang=\"en\">Own work</span>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:CZmarlin\" title=\"User:CZmarlin\">CZmarlin</a> \u2014 Christopher Ziemnowicz, a photo credit would be appreciated if this image is used anywhere other than Wikipedia.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Permission":{"value":"Want to use this image? Please attribute it this way:\n<p>\"Christopher Ziemnowicz (<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\">CC BY 4.0</a>)\" \n<br>\n</p>\nIf you use my image, please leave the URL to it (or a note describing where it is) on my Wikipedia talk page <a class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\">here</a>. Thank you!","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"true","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseUrl":{"value":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Copyrighted":{"value":"True","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"cc-by-sa-4.0","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"150701503":{"pageid":150701503,"ns":6,"title":"File:1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 04of14.jpg","index":5,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":2751302,"width":4032,"height":3024,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":960,"thumbheight":720,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":960,"height":720,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=150701503","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2024-07-23 22:25:57","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 04of14","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Automobile headlamps|Self-published work|Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation|Front views of AMC automobiles|Blue AMC coupes|AMC automobiles in the United States|Photographs by CZmarlin|1974 AMC Javelin|AMO 2024 convention","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"GPSLatitude":{"value":"37.248031","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSLongitude":{"value":"-77.395233","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSMapDatum":{"value":"WGS-84","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin - a \"Blue Diamond\" (paint code - E1) original with a matching interior in blue. 1974 was the last year this high-performance pony was built by American Motors Corporation. The automobile marketplace had changed with the Arab oil embargo and declining interest in high-performance vehicles. In 1974, Chrysler also abandoned the pony car market while the Ford Mustang became a four-cylinder version of the sub-compact Pinto, and GM downsized their standard engines. \n<p>This car has just over 6,000 miles! It is factory-fresh! Original features include AMC's 360 CID (5.9 L) V8 engine, center console with \"stirrup\" shifted automatic transmission, air conditioning, AM/FM stereo radio, and AMC's combination tachometer and clock (Tick-Tach) full instrumentation, sports-type three-spoke steering wheel, and 14-6-inch styled steel road wheels with F70x14 Goodyear Polyglass raised white letter tires. It has the enlarged bumper guard system required for cars sold in California, Georgia, Maryland, and North Carolina. 1974 was the first year of the 5-mph bumper standards, but the Javelin was exempt from incorporating the shock-absorbing bumper systems. \n</p><p>A natural \"diamond\" of a car. The rear  \"ducktail\" spoiler from the AMX model has been added to this car. This AMX design feature was not available on regular Javelins from the factory. Likewise, the Javelins had available \"Rally\" side stripes (optional without the full vinyl body-side scuff molding, which this car has), but the hood \"T\" stripe was available on only the AMX version. \n</p>\n<ul><li>The pictures were taken during the American Motors Owners Association (AMO) annual convention in June 2024, organized by the Potomac Ramblers Club with events held in historic Petersburg downtown by the rail station and at the Keystone Truck and Tractor Museum in Virginia.</li></ul>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2024-06-13 20:54:26","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<span class=\"int-own-work\" lang=\"en\">Own work</span>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:CZmarlin\" title=\"User:CZmarlin\">CZmarlin</a> \u2014 Christopher Ziemnowicz, a photo credit would be appreciated if this image is used anywhere other than Wikipedia.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Permission":{"value":"Want to use this image? Please attribute it this way:\n<p>\"Christopher Ziemnowicz (<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\">CC BY 4.0</a>)\" \n<br>\n</p>\nIf you use my image, please leave the URL to it (or a note describing where it is) on my Wikipedia talk page <a class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\">here</a>. Thank you!","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"true","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseUrl":{"value":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Copyrighted":{"value":"True","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"cc-by-sa-4.0","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"150701513":{"pageid":150701513,"ns":6,"title":"File:1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 09of14.jpg","index":2,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":3694380,"width":4032,"height":3024,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":960,"thumbheight":720,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":960,"height":720,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=150701513","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2024-07-23 22:26:01","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 09of14","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Self-published work|Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation|AMC interiors|Photographs by CZmarlin|Gear sticks (automatic transmission)|1974 AMC Javelin|AMO 2024 convention","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"GPSLatitude":{"value":"37.234311","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSLongitude":{"value":"-77.403078","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSMapDatum":{"value":"WGS-84","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin - a \"Blue Diamond\" (paint code - E1) original with a matching interior in blue. 1974 was the last year this high-performance pony was built by American Motors Corporation. The automobile marketplace had changed with the Arab oil embargo and declining interest in high-performance vehicles. In 1974, Chrysler also abandoned the pony car market while the Ford Mustang became a four-cylinder version of the sub-compact Pinto, and GM downsized their standard engines. \n<p>This car has just over 6,000 miles! It is factory-fresh! Original features include AMC's 360 CID (5.9 L) V8 engine, center console with \"stirrup\" shifted automatic transmission, air conditioning, AM/FM stereo radio, and AMC's combination tachometer and clock (Tick-Tach) full instrumentation, sports-type three-spoke steering wheel, and 14-6-inch styled steel road wheels with F70x14 Goodyear Polyglass raised white letter tires. It has the enlarged bumper guard system required for cars sold in California, Georgia, Maryland, and North Carolina. 1974 was the first year of the 5-mph bumper standards, but the Javelin was exempt from incorporating the shock-absorbing bumper systems. \n</p><p>A natural \"diamond\" of a car! The rear  \"ducktail\" spoiler from the AMX model has been added to this car. This AMX design feature was not available on regular Javelins from the factory. Likewise, the Javelins had available \"Rally\" side stripes (optional without the full vinyl body-side scuff molding, which this car has), but the hood \"T\" stripe was available on only the AMX version. \n</p>\n<ul><li>The pictures were taken during the American Motors Owners Association (AMO) annual convention in June 2024, organized by the Potomac Ramblers Club with events held in historic Petersburg downtown by the rail station and at the Keystone Truck and Tractor Museum in Virginia.</li></ul>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2024-06-12 19:05:22","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<span class=\"int-own-work\" lang=\"en\">Own work</span>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:CZmarlin\" title=\"User:CZmarlin\">CZmarlin</a> \u2014 Christopher Ziemnowicz, a photo credit would be appreciated if this image is used anywhere other than Wikipedia.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Permission":{"value":"Want to use this image? Please attribute it this way:\n<p>\"Christopher Ziemnowicz (<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\">CC BY 4.0</a>)\" \n<br>\n</p>\nIf you use my image, please leave the URL to it (or a note describing where it is) on my Wikipedia talk page <a class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\">here</a>. Thank you!","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"true","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseUrl":{"value":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Copyrighted":{"value":"True","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"cc-by-sa-4.0","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"150701515":{"pageid":150701515,"ns":6,"title":"File:1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 11of14.jpg","index":12,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":2861979,"width":4032,"height":3024,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_11of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_11of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":960,"thumbheight":720,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_11of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_11of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_11of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_11of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":960,"height":720,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_11of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_11of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_11of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_11of14.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=150701515","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2024-07-23 22:26:04","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 11of14","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Self-published work|Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation|Photographs by CZmarlin|1974 AMC Javelin|AMC 360 V8 engine|AMO 2024 convention","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"GPSLatitude":{"value":"37.234292","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSLongitude":{"value":"-77.403053","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSMapDatum":{"value":"WGS-84","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin - a \"Blue Diamond\" (paint code - E1) original with a matching interior in blue. 1974 was the last year this high-performance pony was built by American Motors Corporation. The automobile marketplace had changed with the Arab oil embargo and declining interest in high-performance vehicles. In 1974, Chrysler also abandoned the pony car market while the Ford Mustang became a four-cylinder version of the sub-compact Pinto, and GM downsized their standard engines. \n<p>This car has just over 6,000 miles! It is factory-fresh! Original features include AMC's 360 CID (5.9 L) V8 engine, center console with \"stirrup\" shifted automatic transmission, air conditioning, AM/FM stereo radio, and AMC's combination tachometer and clock (Tick-Tach) full instrumentation, sports-type three-spoke steering wheel, and 14-6-inch styled steel road wheels with F70x14 Goodyear Polyglass raised white letter tires. It has the enlarged bumper guard system required for cars sold in California, Georgia, Maryland, and North Carolina. 1974 was the first year of the 5-mph bumper standards, but the Javelin was exempt from incorporating the shock-absorbing bumper systems. \n</p><p>A natural \"diamond\" of a car! The rear  \"ducktail\" spoiler from the AMX model has been added to this car. This AMX design feature was not available on regular Javelins from the factory. Likewise, the Javelins had available \"Rally\" side stripes (optional without the full vinyl body-side scuff molding, which this car has), but the hood \"T\" stripe was available on only the AMX version. \n</p>\n<ul><li>The pictures were taken during the American Motors Owners Association (AMO) annual convention in June 2024, organized by the Potomac Ramblers Club with events held in historic Petersburg downtown by the rail station and at the Keystone Truck and Tractor Museum in Virginia.</li></ul>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2024-06-12 18:18:23","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<span class=\"int-own-work\" lang=\"en\">Own work</span>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:CZmarlin\" title=\"User:CZmarlin\">CZmarlin</a> \u2014 Christopher Ziemnowicz, a photo credit would be appreciated if this image is used anywhere other than Wikipedia.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Permission":{"value":"Want to use this image? Please attribute it this way:\n<p>\"Christopher Ziemnowicz (<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\">CC BY 4.0</a>)\" \n<br>\n</p>\nIf you use my image, please leave the URL to it (or a note describing where it is) on my Wikipedia talk page <a class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\">here</a>. Thank you!","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"true","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseUrl":{"value":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Copyrighted":{"value":"True","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"cc-by-sa-4.0","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"64093375":{"pageid":64093375,"ns":6,"title":"File:1975 Ford XB Hardtop John Goss Special (37475658932).jpg","index":4,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":4650985,"width":5184,"height":3456,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/1975_Ford_XB_Hardtop_John_Goss_Special_%2837475658932%29.jpg/960px-1975_Ford_XB_Hardtop_John_Goss_Special_%2837475658932%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":960,"thumbheight":640,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/1975_Ford_XB_Hardtop_John_Goss_Special_%2837475658932%29.jpg/1920px-1975_Ford_XB_Hardtop_John_Goss_Special_%2837475658932%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/1975_Ford_XB_Hardtop_John_Goss_Special_%2837475658932%29.jpg/960px-1975_Ford_XB_Hardtop_John_Goss_Special_%2837475658932%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":960,"height":640,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/1975_Ford_XB_Hardtop_John_Goss_Special_%2837475658932%29.jpg/1920px-1975_Ford_XB_Hardtop_John_Goss_Special_%2837475658932%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/16/1975_Ford_XB_Hardtop_John_Goss_Special_%2837475658932%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1975_Ford_XB_Hardtop_John_Goss_Special_(37475658932).jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=64093375","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2017-11-15 22:09:22","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"1975 Ford XB Hardtop John Goss Special (37475658932)","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation|Australia photographs taken on 2017-09-24|Flickr images reviewed by FlickreviewR 2|1975 Ford automobiles (Australia)|Photographs by Sicnag|Ford Falcon 500 (XB)","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"GPSLatitude":{"value":"-34.819391","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSLongitude":{"value":"138.954595","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSMapDatum":{"value":"WGS-84","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<p>Emerald Fire / Polar White. \nThe XB Falcon was built from Sept 1973- July 1976.  \nThe XA got a facelift in 1973; the XB was introduced, it got a more pronounced nose due to the reworking of the bonnet. It also got a divided honeycombgrille, more conventional  taillights and new bumpers. The Falcon now with 4 wheel disc brakes. The GS and GT bonnet got new hood scoops, the design reminiscent of the 1971-73 U.S. Mustang\nEngines; 250 6 or 302 and 351 V8s (now all locally built). \nSedan; Standard, 500, Fairmont and GT\nHardtop: 500, Fairmont, GT and limited John Goss Special\nWagon; Standard, 500 and Fairmont\nAlso Utility and Panel Van in Standard and 500\n</p>\nTo celebrate John Goss winning Bathurst in 1974 this limited edition special was introduced. They were built from August 1975 and just 260 were made. They only came with the 302 Cleveland V8 and available in two unique colour combinations;  Polar White /Apollo Blue also Polar White /Emerald Fire, both with the Orange stripe, large 302 decals and white interiors. They got all the GS features, which included GT dash, steering wheel and bonnet pins, also the GT side scoops and console.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2017-09-24 13:34","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/42220226@N07/37475658932/\">1975 Ford XB Hardtop John Goss Special</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/42220226@N07\">Sicnag</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseS